<BaseLayout title="Car Paint Color Codes — How to Find Your OEM Paint Code" description="Learn how to locate a vehicle's factory paint code, why photo color sampling is not an OEM code, and what information to verify before repainting."><div class="container"><div class="page-hero"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Car Paint Color Codes</div><span class="eyebrow">Automotive color reference</span><h1>How to Find a Car's Factory Paint Code</h1><p>A practical guide to locating and verifying an OEM vehicle paint code before ordering touch-up paint or arranging a repair.</p></div><article class="prose"><div class="notice">A HEX or RGB value sampled from a photograph is not an OEM paint code. Do not use a camera-derived value as a guaranteed paint-mixing formula.</div><h2>Where can a car paint code be located?</h2><p>The location varies by manufacturer, model, model year and market. Common places to check include the driver's door jamb, door pillar, under the hood, trunk area, spare-wheel well or vehicle information label. The code may be labelled Paint, Color, C/TR or appear among other production details. These locations are examples, not a universal rule.</p><h2>What information should you verify?</h2><ul><li>Vehicle manufacturer and exact model.</li><li>Model year and market/region.</li><li>VIN when a dealer or manufacturer lookup requires it.</li><li>The paint code printed on the vehicle label or confirmed by an authorized source.</li></ul><h2>Why does a photo look different from the factory paint?</h2><p>Camera white balance, lighting, reflections, metallic flakes, pearl effects, clear coat, aging and previous repairs can all change the appearance. A photo sampler measures pixels in the image; it does not reverse-engineer the original paint formula.</p><h2>Use ColorCode for visual references</h2><p>You can still use our <a href="/car-color-identifier/">car color identifier</a> to sample a visible digital color, compare areas of a panel and save HEX, RGB and HSL values. For an exact refinish, verify the factory code and consult a professional paint supplier.</p><h2>Related resources</h2><ul><li><a href="/bike-color-identifier/">Bike color identifier</a></li><li><a href="/color-identifier/">Color identifier from camera or photo</a></li><li><a href="/blog/how-to-identify-car-color-from-photo/">How to identify a car color from a photo</a></li></ul></article></div></BaseLayout>
